  {
    "runtag": "uva-gpt5-bm25-debertav3-gpt5mini-nopersonal",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "8-2",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Hi! I'm looking into tattooing 101. Can you tell me how someone should start tattooing as a hobby?",
          "user_rubrik": "How to start tattooing as a hobby?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "great question \u2014 good hygiene and safety are essential from the start. steps to begin tattooing as a hobby safely : 1. learn the basics : study infection control, cross \u2011 contamination, bloodborne viruses, local regulations and consent / record \u2011 keeping requirements. take a recognized training course or workshop. 2. get vaccinated ( tetanus, hepatitis b ) and keep medical checks current. 3. set up a clean workspace : dedicated, easy \u2011 to \u2011 clean surfaces, good lighting, hand \u2011 washing station with single \u2011 use towels, and waste bins for sharps and contaminated disposables. 4. follow strict hand hygiene : wet, soap and lather, scrub thoroughly ( including between fingers and under nails ), rinse, dry with single \u2011 use towels. use alcohol hand rubs as adjunct. 5. use single \u2011 use needles / cartridges, sterilized reusable tools ( autoclave ) if applicable, and disposable barriers. change gloves between clients and after touching non \u2011 sterile surfaces. 6. practice technique on synthetic skin, pig skin alternatives, or through an apprenticeship before tattooing people. 7. conduct risk assessments : identify hazards, set control points ( sterilisation, ppe,",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0030-05-17024:0": 26.75189971923828,
            "clueweb22-en0023-07-00305:0": 24.49650001525879,
            "clueweb22-en0003-00-00231:0": 24.434200286865234,
            "clueweb22-en0002-09-13235:1": 24.25749969482422,
            "clueweb22-en0010-60-06985:3": 23.422000885009766
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that\u2019s super helpful! What specific equipment and materials are actually needed to set up for tattooing, like machines, inks, etc.?",
          "user_rubrik": "What equipments are needed to start tattooing?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "nice \u2014 here \u2019 s a compact checklist of the specific equipment and materials you \u2019 ll need to set up safely and practically : machines & power - tattoo machines : rotary ( pen ) and / or coil ( liners & shaders ) - reliable power supply ( digital lcd preferred ), foot pedal, clip cord ( rca or 3. 5mm ) or wireless battery pack needles & cartridges - disposable sterile cartridges / needles : liners ( rl ), shaders / round shaders ( rs ), mags / flat ( rm, m1 ) - assorted sizes ( eg. 3rl, 5rl, 7rl, 9rl, 5m1, 7m1 ) grips, tips & hardware - disposable grips or reusable aluminium grips, tubes / tips, grommets, o \u2011 rings, rubber bands, grip tape - adjustment tools for machines inks & ink handling - professional tattoo inks ( variety of colors ), ink caps ( mixed sizes ), stainless ink cup holder, ink - mixing sticks hygiene & sterilization - disposable gloves, antibacterial soap, green soap, antiseptic wipes, surface disinfectant - sharps",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0032-43-06101:5": 63.89120101928711,
            "clueweb22-en0017-62-10056:0": 63.74919891357422,
            "clueweb22-en0029-73-00912:0": 62.172698974609375,
            "clueweb22-en0006-20-04480:0": 61.61159896850586,
            "clueweb22-en0024-21-00136:15": 61.30970001220703
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s a detailed list, thanks! Now I\u2019m curious: what different tattoo needle types exist and what are each used for?",
          "user_rubrik": "What different tattoo needle types exist?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "short primer \u2014 common needle types and when to use each : - round liner ( rl ) : needles in a tight circular cluster. for crisp lines, lettering and fine detail. sizes like 3rl, 5rl \u2014 lower number = finer line. - round shader ( rs ) / round magnum ( rt ) : looser round groupings used for soft shading, small color packing, and gradients. - magnum ( m, rm, m1, cm ) : needles stacked in two rows ( magnums ) or single - stack ( m1 ). best for packing color and large - area shading. curved magnums ( cm ) give softer gradients and less skin trauma. m1 ( single - stack ) is thinner than standard magnums for more control. - flat ( f ) : needles in a single straight row. good for crisp edges, geometric fills and certain blending techniques. - bugpin / fine needles : smaller - diameter tips within any configuration for ultra - fine detail and very smooth soft shading. notes : needle count ( 3, 5, 7, 9 \u2026 ) controls coverage ; grouping tightness ( tight vs loose ) affects how much ink is deposited. always use pre - sterilized, single \u2011 use needles",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0015-21-00443:7": 50.16680145263672,
            "clueweb22-en0008-01-12699:8": 48.507598876953125,
            "clueweb22-en0015-21-00443:5": 48.3119010925293,
            "clueweb22-en0015-21-00443:6": 47.92689895629883,
            "clueweb22-en0015-21-00443:4": 47.740699768066406
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That's neat, thanks! Where do people usually buy tattoo needles? Are there trusted shops or websites for supplies?",
          "user_rubrik": "Where to buy tattoo needles?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "People buy needles from a few reliable sources \u2014 but always choose reputable sellers and pre\u2011sterilized, individually packaged product.  Where to buy - Official brand stores/websites (Cheyenne, Dragonhawk, YILONG, Mellie, etc.) - Established tattoo-supply retailers (online pro suppliers and local licensed stores) - Major marketplaces (Amazon) but buy from the brand or an authorized seller with good reviews - Tattoo conventions or trusted local shops (you can inspect packaging)  What to check before buying - Pre\u2011sterilized (EO gas), individually sealed, with lot number and expiry - Safety membrane / medical\u2011grade tips for cartridges if using pen-style machines - Compatibility with your machine (cartridge vs standard tube/needle) - Single\u2011use disposables only; avoid repackaged/unknown sellers - Ask for a sterilization certificate if in doubt  Buying from known brands and licensed supply shops gives the best mix of safety and reliability.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0040-53-18168:0": 42.38629913330078,
            "clueweb22-en0015-60-03031:3": 41.516998291015625,
            "clueweb22-en0008-01-12699:4": 40.58219909667969,
            "clueweb22-en0025-66-04926:6": 40.08340072631836,
            "clueweb22-en0015-60-03031:6": 39.80690002441406
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That helps\u2014thanks! I heard about REACH compliance in tattoo supplies. What does REACH compliant mean for tattoo needles or inks?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is REACH compliant in tattoo needles?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "REACH\u2011compliant means the product and any chemicals in it meet EU rules on registration, evaluation, authorization and restriction of chemicals. For tattoo supplies that typically means:  - Inks (mixtures): manufacturers/importers must register substances if they import/manufacture above thresholds, supply proper Safety Data Sheets (SDS/CLP), and respect any substance restrictions or authorizations. Some pigments/SVHCs may be restricted or need prior authorization. - Needles (articles): usually exempt unless they (a) intentionally release a chemical in normal use or disposal, or (b) contain a Substance of Very High Concern (SVHC) above 0.1% w/w (assessment is based on amount likely released). If affected, suppliers must inform downstream users and comply with notification/registration rules.  What to ask suppliers: SDS for inks, written REACH/SVHC declaration for inks and needles, CLP labels, and proof of supplier registration/authorization if relevant. Prefer EU\u2011based reputable suppliers to reduce compliance risk.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0006-77-11966:2": 57.3223991394043,
            "clueweb22-en0006-77-11966:1": 55.88119888305664,
            "clueweb22-en0041-85-19451:2": 48.70600128173828,
            "clueweb22-en0031-04-01682:0": 48.33039855957031,
            "clueweb22-en0008-84-09619:2": 48.301300048828125
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Got it, that fits with what I heard here in Germany. Are there learning resources that explain tattooing basics, like online courses, books, or videos?",
          "user_rubrik": "What learning resources are available about tattooing?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "yes \u2014 mix structured learning, reliable references, and hands \u2011 on practice. practical path and resources to look for : - formal training : local apprenticeship or an accredited short course ( infection control, bloodborne pathogens, consent / record \u2011 keeping ). check your local health authority for mandatory training in germany. - hygiene & safety courses : mandatory in many places ; look for certified bbp / sterilisation classes. - in \u2011 person workshops : day / weekend masterclasses at reputable studios for supervised practice. - books & e \u2011 books : beginner tattoo texts covering equipment, needle theory, skin anatomy, and aftercare ( use up \u2011 to \u2011 date, well \u2011 reviewed titles ). - high \u2011 quality videos : paid tutorial series or youtube channels run by established tattooists \u2014 prioritise creators with studio credentials and good reviews. - supplier / manufacturer training : many reputable equipment / ink brands offer tutorials and sds / handling guides. - practice materials : synthetic skin, practice kits, and technique drills before working on people. - community learning : forums, local conventions, and mentors for critique and industry contacts. start with certified hygiene training and an apprenticeship or supervised practice \u2014 combine reading and videos, then lots of guided",
