  {
    "runtag": "uva-gpt5mini-bm25-debertav3-gpt5mini",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "8-2",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I'm just curious about tattooing as a hobby. How do people usually start with it?",
          "user_rubrik": "How to start tattooing as a hobby?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Great hobby choice \u2014 many artists start the same way. A simple roadmap:  - Build drawing skills and design vocabulary (your drawing ability is a big plus). Start with small, minimal designs.   - Learn safety and hygiene first: take a basic course (online or local) covering sterilisation (autoclave/ultrasonic), cleaning, cross-contamination, and client aftercare.   - Buy a beginner tattoo kit and practise on fake skin or fruit. Start with hand-poked or simple machine work before moving to real skin.   - Never tattoo strangers without supervision. Get feedback from experienced artists and practise following strict cleanliness protocols.   - Seek an apprenticeship \u2014 it\u2019s the fastest way to learn real client work, machine handling, and studio workflow (often unpaid but invaluable).   - Check local regulations and licensing requirements in Germany before offering paid work.  Take it slow: safety and technique matter more than speed.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0045-06-05348:0": -4.961584091186523,
            "clueweb22-en0046-85-00369:2": -5.037276268005371,
            "clueweb22-en0045-06-05348:1": -5.268881797790527,
            "clueweb22-en0022-31-15676:15": -5.623184680938721,
            "clueweb22-en0024-22-17473:7": -6.169189453125
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks! What specific equipment do beginners need to buy for tattooing?",
          "user_rubrik": "What equipments are needed to start tattooing?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "good question \u2014 here \u2019 s a compact starter shopping list and why each item matters : - tattoo machine : rotary / pen ( easy for beginners ) or a hand \u2011 poke kit if you want stick \u2011 n \u2011 poke. - power supply + foot pedal ( or rechargeable wireless battery if you prefer cordless ). - sterile, single \u2011 use needles / cartridges in common sizes ( 3rl, 5rl, shaders ) and disposable grips / tubes ( or autoclavable gear + autoclave if reusing ). - quality tattoo inks from a reputable brand ( starter black + a few colors ). - ink caps, disposable pigment cups, and small disposable syringes for ink mixing. - transfer / stencil paper and a fine marker for freehand. - practice skin sheets and fruit for technique practice. - ppe & hygiene : nitrile gloves, barrier film, surface disinfectant, green soap, isopropyl alcohol, disposable razors. - sharps container and biohazard waste bags. - aftercare ointment and client consent / aftercare forms. buy from reputable suppliers ( not the cheapest",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0013-41-04803:0": 0.5823280811309814,
            "clueweb22-en0027-86-19967:0": 0.07779807597398758,
            "clueweb22-en0004-09-01120:0": -0.06124391034245491,
            "clueweb22-en0021-73-10825:0": -0.14903706312179565,
            "clueweb22-en0007-03-16021:0": -0.6180611848831177
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "What different tattoo needle types exist?",
          "user_rubrik": "What different tattoo needle types exist?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Short answer \u2014 lots of varieties. Common types and what they\u2019re used for:  - Round Liner (RL): tightly grouped round needles for crisp lines and dotwork.   - Round Shader (RS): a looser round grouping for soft shading and small fills.   - Magnum (M, M1, RM): multiple needles stacked in two rows (stacked or weaved) for shading and packing color over larger areas.   - Curved Magnum (CM or curved RM): mags arranged with a slight curve to hug contours and produce smoother blends.   - Flat (F): a single straight row\u2014good for packing color, block shading, and some bold lines.   - Weaved/Stacked/Weave variations: different mag layouts that change how ink lays down (softer vs denser).   - Bugpin/Small-gauge needles: thinner needles for very fine detail and subtle realism.   - Cartridges vs loose needles: cartridge systems (snap\u2011in) vs traditional needle bars/tubes\u2014functionally the same needle types in different formats.   Also note taper (short/long) and gauge (thickness) affect how a needle feels and deposits ink.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0016-40-05463:0": 1.0116063356399536,
            "clueweb22-en0013-41-04803:0": 0.8412538766860962,
            "clueweb22-en0020-34-02490:0": 0.7505401372909546,
            "clueweb22-en0031-20-16817:0": 0.691038966178894,
            "clueweb22-en0022-58-06316:0": 0.3107050061225891
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Where do people typically buy tattoo needles in Germany?",
          "user_rubrik": "Where to buy tattoo needles?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Typical places people buy needles in Germany:  - Tattoo supply shops (local storefronts in larger cities) \u2014 good for seeing products and asking staff.   - Specialized online retailers (EU/German suppliers that sell professional, sterile cartridges and loose needles).   - Large online marketplaces (convenient, but verify seller/reviews and product certifications).   - Professional distributors/wholesalers (used by studios \u2014 best for bulk or higher\u2011end brands).   - Tattoo conventions, trade shows, or directly through your apprenticeship/studio (experienced artists often recommend trusted brands).  Safety tips: only buy sterile, single\u2011use needles or CE\u2011marked cartridges with clear sterilisation indicators and expiry dates. Match needle format (cartridge vs traditional bar/tube) to your machine. Avoid the cheapest unlabelled imports \u2014 ask a mentor or shop for trusted brands and compliance with German/EU rules before using on skin.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0013-41-04803:0": 0.8148133754730225,
            "clueweb22-en0031-20-16817:0": 0.7174635529518127,
            "clueweb22-en0022-58-06316:0": 0.37953540682792664,
            "clueweb22-en0027-86-19967:0": 0.2861328125,
